  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}=-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-(-1)^{2}/2}={e}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =-(1){e}^{-(1)^{2}/2}=-{e}^{-1/2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
